{"id":"8e31519c07a2039e99b07566a3e3aa6d8e82396a956a53cedb9f4c697f5947ec","pubkey":"08429d37bdf2f0e0d96bed7dd8a09ebaba4748dec96fff926cf39397471d2ea7","created_at":1792145350,"kind":1,"tags":[],"content":"Elven words for jewels\n\nElven words for jewels\nElven words for metals, for stones\nThese are elven words for jewels and gemstones including poetic and archaic words.\nMore in the linguistic resources and word of the day (wotd) tags! Let me know if there are any topics you would like to see covered\nMírë in Quenya and Mîr in Sindarin are the primary words for jewel or gem. Both derive from Mir meaning precious, esteem or value\nLúlë or luilë are the Quenya words for sapphire. It is related to lūnë, an older word meaning deep blue\nMalicon (Q) means amber. It is derived from mala meaning yellow\nMarilla is an older Quenya word for pearl. It drives from Mara meaning sand or soil.\nBrithla means pearl in old Sindarin.\nNindon means lapis lazuli in Quenya. It derives from nin meaning blueness or blue green and ond meaning stone.\nNyelecca in Quenya means onyx. It derives from a word for nail or hard.\nSercëmirë means blood gem for ruby in Quenya. Caranmîr means ruby in Sindarin, translating to (deep) red stone\nCalenmîr in Sindarin and laicamirë in Quenya mean emerald. Both translate to bright green stone.\nNote: the last two points are neologisms! Let me know if there’s any others you want me to make!\n\nhttps://outofangband.tumblr.com/post/830015836153610240","sig":"628e1966bf7775921405a7861f824a25c17e2fd4375dda07c7a884c65cdc0dee95e6ecb7e2b4ef6fa0db8b6778d2ddf4145865f41df1be8576393dd34b0ab0d1"}
